  ["sources", "Sources"],
];

const faqs = [
  { q: "How much can I borrow against 1kg of gold in Australia?", a: "There is no single published percentage that applies to every 1kg bar. At 11:15am on 14 August 2026, The Perth Mint showed a “Perth Mint Buys” price of $192,004.95 for a 1kg cast gold bar. If that figure were also the lender's assessed collateral value and a 60% loan-to-value ratio were applied, the arithmetic would be about $115,203. This is an illustration only: ALYRA does not publish 60% as its gold-bullion lending policy or as an expected offer." },
  { q: "What percentage of my gold value can I borrow?", a: "ALYRA does not publish a fixed gold-bullion loan-to-value percentage. Its published process says the amount depends on the bullion and its suitability as security after checks of weight, purity, refiner, ownership, authenticity and current market pricing. The percentage and resulting amount are therefore determined by the specific assessment and written offer." },
  { q: "Is a gold-backed loan based on the spot price?", a: "Spot price can be a starting reference, but it is not the same as the loan amount. A lender may use spot, an LBMA benchmark, a dealer bid or a mint buy price to inform market value, then apply collateral adjustments and a loan-to-value ratio." },
  { q: "Can I borrow against Perth Mint gold bars?", a: "ALYRA says it considers recognised investment-grade bars, subject to verification and approval. The Perth Mint's 1kg cast gold bar is 99.99% pure and stamped with its mark, weight, purity, production date and serial number, but acceptance and loan amount still depend on the full assessment." },
  { q: "Can I borrow against gold coins?", a: "Yes. ALYRA says recognised investment and collectible gold coins may be considered, subject to verification of weight, purity, condition and authenticity. Its published page says value is based on verified weight and purity with reference to current market pricing, alongside any collectible premium for recognised coins; the amount available still depends on the full assessment." },
  { q: "Do I keep ownership of my gold while I borrow?", a: "Under ALYRA's published process, you remain the owner while the bullion is held as security, and the gold is returned after the loan is repaid under the agreed terms. The asset is nevertheless at risk if repayment obligations are not met." },
  { q: "What purity does gold need to be for a bullion loan?", a: "ALYRA does not publish a single minimum fineness for every private bullion loan; it verifies the purity of each holding. As market references, The Perth Mint's 1kg cast bar is 99.99% pure, while LBMA Good Delivery gold bars must meet a minimum fineness of 995 parts per thousand. These are market specifications, not a published ALYRA minimum." },
  { q: "What documents should I provide with my gold?", a: "Useful records include purchase invoices, receipts, assay certificates where available, serial numbers, bar lists and storage or custody statements. They help the lender verify identity, ownership and authenticity and can reduce the need for additional checks." },
  { q: "What happens if the gold price falls after I take the loan?", a: "A lower gold price can reduce collateral value, but the effect on an existing loan depends on the written terms. ALYRA's general collateral guide notes that some facilities may require repayment, additional collateral or partial sale if collateral value falls, depending on the loan terms. ALYRA's public gold-bullion page does not state a standard automatic trigger, so the written provisions dealing with collateral value, repayment and default are what matter." },
  { q: "What happens if I do not repay a gold-backed pawn loan?", a: "Your pledged gold is at risk if you do not meet the repayment obligations. For a Queensland pawn transaction, the statutory redemption period is three months from the day the property is pawned unless a longer period is agreed; after the period expires, the Act deals with unredeemed property and the circumstances in which it may be sold. Other transaction structures or jurisdictions may differ, so the written terms and applicable law matter." },
];

const crumbs = [
